1
00:00:09,447 --> 00:00:15,302
All right, let's jump into some basics for
network security.

2
00:00:15,302 --> 00:00:20,706
And so this is going to be the different
techniques that we'll see come up

3
00:00:20,706 --> 00:00:25,744
in those various solutions that we
discussed in the last video and

4
00:00:25,744 --> 00:00:30,342
that we'll go through in detail
in the subsequent videos.

5
00:00:30,342 --> 00:00:33,497
All right, so
starting with confidentiality.

6
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:41,276
So a reminder what this means is what we
want is to be able to send a message and

7
00:00:41,276 --> 00:00:46,847
keep it secret from anyone
other than the recipient.

8
00:00:46,847 --> 00:00:49,877
Now, to understand this,

9
00:00:49,877 --> 00:00:55,822
let's first talk about
a little bit of terminology.

10
00:00:55,822 --> 00:01:01,931
So when we say there's two
parties that are communicating,

11
00:01:01,931 --> 00:01:06,501
we commonly give them names,
Alice and Bob.

12
00:01:06,501 --> 00:01:11,313
So you'll see that a lot in
kind of network security.

13
00:01:11,313 --> 00:01:17,175
Then any attacker we'll see
will also be given a name.

14
00:01:17,175 --> 00:01:21,684
And so here we're concerned
about eavesdropping, so

15
00:01:21,684 --> 00:01:23,847
we name the attacker Eve.

16
00:01:23,847 --> 00:01:28,286
And so some other names you
might see might be Eve or

17
00:01:28,286 --> 00:01:32,840
Mallory or Trudy and
it all depends on the context.

18
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:37,832
But in general,
there's some third party that's not

19
00:01:37,832 --> 00:01:42,834
part of that communication and
shown as the attacker.

20
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:47,297
We then want to be able
to transmit the data.

21
00:01:47,297 --> 00:01:51,374
And so in this, the unencrypted traffic,

22
00:01:51,374 --> 00:01:58,296
the one that is what Alice is sending
directly is what we call plain text.

23
00:01:58,296 --> 00:02:04,472
So that is what we don't want Eve
in this example to be able to see.

24
00:02:04,472 --> 00:02:11,351
So we then use an encryption algorithm to
change that into what we call ciphertext,

25
00:02:11,351 --> 00:02:17,757
which is, we don't mind if Eve can see
that because it has to them no meaning.

26
00:02:17,757 --> 00:02:22,234
But then through a decryption algorithm,

27
00:02:22,234 --> 00:02:27,341
it can be converted back
into plain text for Bob.

28
00:02:27,341 --> 00:02:32,224
And then the last kind of
bit is some information that

29
00:02:32,224 --> 00:02:35,887
is needed as part of this encryption and

30
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:40,551
decryption process
are something known as keys.

31
00:02:40,551 --> 00:02:45,002
So here it's showing that Alice
has an encryption key and

32
00:02:45,002 --> 00:02:50,584
Bob has a decryption key and those
are used as part of those algorithms.

33
00:02:50,584 --> 00:02:54,231
So we'll go over a little
more what those keys

34
00:02:54,231 --> 00:02:58,369
are because there's a couple
of different types.

35
00:02:58,369 --> 00:03:03,845
And then you might see in
the bottom left this kind

36
00:03:03,845 --> 00:03:11,994
of terminology where we might use M to
indicate the plain text message and

37
00:03:11,994 --> 00:03:16,284
then K subscript A of M parentheses M.

38
00:03:16,284 --> 00:03:22,928
And that's indicating some ciphertext
that's been encrypted with key KA.

39
00:03:22,928 --> 00:03:29,940
And then to decrypt it, you can take
that message, this ciphertext message,

40
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:35,376
the KA of M, wrap that, and
now we see KB being applied to it.

41
00:03:35,376 --> 00:03:40,175
So that's applying key B or key B, and

42
00:03:40,175 --> 00:03:44,227
then we can get back to message.

43
00:03:44,227 --> 00:03:47,310
All right, so
now in terms of what these keys are.

44
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:53,276
So the first type of encryption
is symmetric encryption.

45
00:03:53,276 --> 00:03:56,870
And this is where there's a shared key.

46
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:01,381
So all a key is some bit pattern that was

47
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:05,754
generated through some mechanism.

48
00:04:05,754 --> 00:04:10,644
And in this case, Alice and
Bob share this key and

49
00:04:10,644 --> 00:04:15,667
that's used in both encryption and
decryption.

50
00:04:15,667 --> 00:04:20,696
So the properties of this,
when we have symmetric

51
00:04:20,696 --> 00:04:26,326
encryption is that it's fast
to encrypt and decrypt but

52
00:04:26,326 --> 00:04:33,185
the challenge here is that you need
a way to be able to share this key.

53
00:04:33,185 --> 00:04:38,840
So as a trivial example, we're
going to use XOR as our encryption and

54
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:40,969
decryption algorithm.

55
00:04:40,969 --> 00:04:47,334
So we're going to have a shared key
with this bit pattern as above.

56
00:04:47,334 --> 00:04:54,090
And let's say we want to transmit
the word Eric in ASCII characters,

57
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:58,012
and then we want to be able to receive it.

58
00:04:58,012 --> 00:05:00,551
All right, so
let's look at just the first character.

59
00:05:00,551 --> 00:05:05,335
If we do it block by block,
where each block is eight bits and

60
00:05:05,335 --> 00:05:09,664
the key is also eight bits,
and the algorithm is XOR.

61
00:05:09,664 --> 00:05:15,943
So in hex, the character e
in ASCII would be in hex 45,

62
00:05:15,943 --> 00:05:20,753
or if we've wrote it out in binary 0100

63
00:05:20,753 --> 00:05:25,181
0101 and so that's the capital E.

64
00:05:25,181 --> 00:05:31,790
And then we can XOR it
with the key as down here.

65
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:37,759
And if you recall, XOR one,
XOR with one is a zero.

66
00:05:37,759 --> 00:05:42,281
Zero XOR with one is one,
one XOR with one is zero,

67
00:05:42,281 --> 00:05:47,154
zero XOR with zero, zero,
and so forth, all right?

68
00:05:47,154 --> 00:05:53,611
And so what we end up with
is the hex value 92 or 9,2.

69
00:05:53,611 --> 00:06:00,508
And if you convert that to ASCII,
that would be that apostrophe, all right?

70
00:06:00,508 --> 00:06:02,267
So that's on the encrypt side.

71
00:06:02,267 --> 00:06:06,586
So if we continued all the characters
these would be the symbols,

72
00:06:06,586 --> 00:06:09,656
the ASCII symbols that
would be transmitted.

73
00:06:09,656 --> 00:06:11,615
And then we can apply XOR.

74
00:06:11,615 --> 00:06:16,044
So now we take that 92 or
the apostrophe and

75
00:06:16,044 --> 00:06:19,399
run it through this decryption,

76
00:06:19,399 --> 00:06:23,854
which is in this case
xoring it with the key.

77
00:06:23,854 --> 00:06:29,294
And we can see that it ends up with 4,5 or

78
00:06:29,294 --> 00:06:33,504
ASCII character E, all right?

79
00:06:33,504 --> 00:06:38,256
So you can see this is a simple example.

80
00:06:38,256 --> 00:06:43,803
This wouldn't be too secure,
but a better example

81
00:06:43,803 --> 00:06:49,103
is a protocol called or
an algorithm called AES.

82
00:06:49,103 --> 00:06:54,245
This has been a standard
from NIST since 2001.

83
00:06:54,245 --> 00:07:00,062
And you can use 128, 192, 256 bit keys.

84
00:07:00,062 --> 00:07:04,777
And if you go through
the details which we won't,

85
00:07:04,777 --> 00:07:08,817
it's similar in spirit to kind of XOR, but

86
00:07:08,817 --> 00:07:15,109
a lot more complex operations
where we're substituting bytes and

87
00:07:15,109 --> 00:07:20,612
shifting different rows and
mixing up the bytes of data and

88
00:07:20,612 --> 00:07:25,693
using different parts of
the key in different areas.

89
00:07:25,693 --> 00:07:29,370
But it's basically you
take the plain text,

90
00:07:29,370 --> 00:07:34,692
run it through this algorithm
with the key, you get ciphertext,

91
00:07:34,692 --> 00:07:39,561
and then you run it in reverse and
you can get the plain text.

92
00:07:39,561 --> 00:07:46,110
All right, so the other kind of encryption
is called asymmetric encryption.

93
00:07:46,110 --> 00:07:49,595
And so this is where one of the parties,

94
00:07:49,595 --> 00:07:54,041
in this case it's Bob,
will generate a key pair.

95
00:07:54,041 --> 00:08:00,211
One, Bob will keep private, and
then the other one, Bob can share.

96
00:08:02,074 --> 00:08:06,872
With anybody, it doesn't matter who
has it, so it's Bob's Public key.

97
00:08:06,872 --> 00:08:12,884
And what this can do is the way
these algorithms are constructed

98
00:08:12,884 --> 00:08:20,540
is you can use Bob's public key to do
the encryption to create the ciphertext.

99
00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:27,374
Now, the only person that could decrypt
it is Bob, who has the private key.

100
00:08:27,374 --> 00:08:30,708
So you need that private
key to do the decryption.

101
00:08:30,708 --> 00:08:35,833
Now, this is slow and
we'll see it can only really handles

102
00:08:35,833 --> 00:08:40,786
shorter messages, but
it's simple to share the key.

103
00:08:40,786 --> 00:08:47,120
You can share this public key with
anybody, doesn't matter who has it.

104
00:08:48,254 --> 00:08:54,200
So the common algorithm, or a popular
algorithm here is something called RSA for

105
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:56,659
the encryption and decryption.

106
00:08:56,659 --> 00:08:59,840
So given a public key and

107
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:04,300
a private key, n and e and n and d.

108
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:07,521
So e for encryption, d for decryption.

109
00:09:07,521 --> 00:09:12,651
In that case,
message m is just a bit pattern

110
00:09:12,651 --> 00:09:17,428
that can be interpreted as an integer.

111
00:09:17,428 --> 00:09:20,193
So to encrypt the message,

112
00:09:20,193 --> 00:09:25,483
you can take the message,
which is just an integer,

113
00:09:25,483 --> 00:09:30,410
raise it to the power of
whatever that key is, e,

114
00:09:30,410 --> 00:09:35,640
and then do mod n and
that becomes the ciphertext.

115
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:38,904
And then you do a similar process,

116
00:09:38,904 --> 00:09:44,662
you take that ciphertext,
raise it to d, and then do mod n.

117
00:09:44,662 --> 00:09:52,300
And this works because of the properties
of mod and how n, e, and d are chosen.

118
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:56,180
And this is briefly
illustrated on the next slide.

119
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:59,450
So here is an example.

120
00:09:59,450 --> 00:10:04,824
So let's say, as part of this
generating what e and d are,

121
00:10:04,824 --> 00:10:11,514
you choose some values, p and q, and
that ends up kind of resulting in n,

122
00:10:11,514 --> 00:10:16,622
which is 35,
which is the multiplication of those.

123
00:10:16,622 --> 00:10:22,272
And then another factor that you use is z,
which is p-1 and

124
00:10:22,272 --> 00:10:26,909
q-1 multiply those together and
you get 24.

125
00:10:26,909 --> 00:10:29,592
So the end result,

126
00:10:29,592 --> 00:10:34,959
if you were to go through the process,

127
00:10:34,959 --> 00:10:40,659
you get an encryption key or
the e is 5 and

128
00:10:40,659 --> 00:10:44,537
then d is 29, all right?

129
00:10:44,537 --> 00:10:49,535
So with those, let's say we're
encrypting an 8 bit message.

130
00:10:49,535 --> 00:10:52,769
We have this bit pattern right there.

131
00:10:52,769 --> 00:10:58,007
If we convert that to a number,
an integer, it's 12.

132
00:10:58,007 --> 00:11:02,587
So then we do 12 raised to the power of 5.

133
00:11:02,587 --> 00:11:07,129
And this is what we would get,

134
00:11:07,129 --> 00:11:12,047
24,832, and then mod n.

135
00:11:12,047 --> 00:11:17,234
So since n was 35,
we mod it and we get 17.

136
00:11:18,387 --> 00:11:23,562
So now we can take in the decrypt side,
take 17,

137
00:11:23,562 --> 00:11:28,491
raise it to d, and
you get a really long number,

138
00:11:28,491 --> 00:11:32,942
and then mod that by n and
then you get 12.

139
00:11:32,942 --> 00:11:37,144
And you can see that these two
values are the same, and so

140
00:11:37,144 --> 00:11:42,081
this is just an example of going
through the math and how it works.

141
00:11:42,081 --> 00:11:45,681
And I know we glossed
over kind of how e and d.

142
00:11:45,681 --> 00:11:50,365
So there's certainly
documentation out there

143
00:11:50,365 --> 00:11:55,174
that you can look at to
get the concrete details.

144
00:11:55,174 --> 00:12:01,250
But this gives you an overview
of kind of why it's slow,

145
00:12:01,250 --> 00:12:06,034
why it only works for
smaller bit patterns,

146
00:12:06,034 --> 00:12:09,152
because you have to do this.

147
00:12:09,152 --> 00:12:12,353
All right, so
now we saw these two properties.

148
00:12:12,353 --> 00:12:18,749
We have this shared key, which is faster,
can handle longer messages.

149
00:12:18,749 --> 00:12:23,379
We have the public,
the asymmetric encryption which can

150
00:12:23,379 --> 00:12:28,432
handle shorter messages, but
it's easier to distribute.

151
00:12:28,432 --> 00:12:34,057
So a lot of times you
can use the asymmetric

152
00:12:34,057 --> 00:12:40,622
encryption to be able to
exchange a shared key.

153
00:12:40,622 --> 00:12:46,538
And here it's being called the session
key, because it's just created just for

154
00:12:46,538 --> 00:12:50,818
the communication between Alice and
Bob for right now.

155
00:12:50,818 --> 00:12:54,398
So the part one of this is that Bob or

156
00:12:54,398 --> 00:12:58,472
that Alice picks some session key, and

157
00:12:58,472 --> 00:13:04,556
then uses Bob's public key to
encrypt it and send to Bob.

158
00:13:04,556 --> 00:13:09,933
And then Bob is the only one That
can decrypt it with its private key.

159
00:13:09,933 --> 00:13:13,821
So that the wording here, there's a typo,
that should say public key,

160
00:13:13,821 --> 00:13:15,628
but you can see it in the picture.

161
00:13:15,628 --> 00:13:19,566
It's using the Bob's public key,

162
00:13:19,566 --> 00:13:24,429
encrypted session key and
now Bob has that.

163
00:13:24,429 --> 00:13:30,477
And so now, since they're the only
two that know the session key,

164
00:13:30,477 --> 00:13:35,769
because Alice picked it, and
then Bob was the only one that

165
00:13:35,769 --> 00:13:42,681
could decrypt the message that included
it, now they can use a shared key and

166
00:13:42,681 --> 00:13:48,518
use symmetric encryption in
the actual longer communication.

167
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:54,660
All right, so
the next property is message integrity.

168
00:13:54,660 --> 00:13:59,088
So here, we don't want this attacker,

169
00:13:59,088 --> 00:14:05,120
we don't want the messages
to be able to be modified.

170
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:09,397
So in this case, Alice sends something,
sends a message, but

171
00:14:09,397 --> 00:14:13,722
then what Bob is actually
receiving is a different message.

172
00:14:13,722 --> 00:14:19,082
And so we need a know to be able
to ensure that at a minimum,

173
00:14:19,082 --> 00:14:23,124
Bob knows that it's a different message.

174
00:14:23,124 --> 00:14:28,240
So now, starting off,
we're going to use hashing.

175
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:33,450
So a hash function is something
where you can calculate

176
00:14:33,450 --> 00:14:39,440
a fixed length digest from
a variable length message.

177
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:44,597
And a desired property here is that,
it's computationally

178
00:14:44,597 --> 00:14:51,620
infeasible to find another message
that would result in the same digest.

179
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:56,975
Now, it assumes that the message

180
00:14:56,975 --> 00:15:00,806
digest is unmodified.

181
00:15:00,806 --> 00:15:06,549
And because of this, this is something
that's useful in integrity checking,

182
00:15:06,549 --> 00:15:08,596
like software downloads.

183
00:15:08,596 --> 00:15:15,223
So you go to a website, you're downloading
some software on that website,

184
00:15:15,223 --> 00:15:20,220
they'll list out some
hash number that digest.

185
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:23,296
And so that you can, when you download it,

186
00:15:23,296 --> 00:15:26,641
you run the same hash
algorithm to then check.

187
00:15:26,641 --> 00:15:33,809
So here's your downloaded software,
you run that same hash on it,

188
00:15:33,809 --> 00:15:40,221
compare it to what that digest is,
and if they're the same,

189
00:15:40,221 --> 00:15:45,281
then there wasn't any
corruption in the data.

190
00:15:45,281 --> 00:15:53,724
All right, but this transfer
the message digest is a problem.

191
00:15:53,724 --> 00:15:59,990
And so here,
this message authentication code.

192
00:16:01,815 --> 00:16:07,901
Particularly hash-keyed
one removes the assumption

193
00:16:07,901 --> 00:16:12,402
that the digest is known or unmodified.

194
00:16:12,402 --> 00:16:18,134
And so here the sender and
receiver share a secret key,

195
00:16:18,134 --> 00:16:26,491
and that secret key is used as part of
what's being used in the hashing, right?

196
00:16:26,491 --> 00:16:32,239
So here we can append well, so
this is how you would calculate it.

197
00:16:32,239 --> 00:16:35,152
So you would take a hash of the key,

198
00:16:35,152 --> 00:16:40,176
concatenated with the message,
and then you hash the key,

199
00:16:40,176 --> 00:16:45,422
concatenated with that hash,
and that's what you can send.

200
00:16:45,422 --> 00:16:48,772
Now, it would be challenging for

201
00:16:48,772 --> 00:16:53,679
anybody to message or
modify both the message and

202
00:16:53,679 --> 00:16:58,946
this Mac code,
this message authentication code,

203
00:16:58,946 --> 00:17:06,726
in the same way such that now when you
perform this calculation of hashing it,

204
00:17:06,726 --> 00:17:13,688
comparing it to what you received and
saying that they're the same.

205
00:17:13,688 --> 00:17:18,985
All right, so this allows you this
provides that kind of protection

206
00:17:18,985 --> 00:17:24,565
where it would have been previously
easy to modify both the message and

207
00:17:24,565 --> 00:17:29,312
the digest if you're in
the middle of that communication.

208
00:17:32,175 --> 00:17:39,405
All right, so
the next thing is a Digital Signature.

209
00:17:39,405 --> 00:17:44,391
So this is where you
can use asymmetric keys

210
00:17:44,391 --> 00:17:49,516
to enable checking that the message hasn't

211
00:17:49,516 --> 00:17:53,821
changed and that it came from Alice.

212
00:17:53,821 --> 00:17:59,527
So here Alice would use
her private key to "sign",

213
00:17:59,527 --> 00:18:02,510
encrypt effectively, and

214
00:18:02,510 --> 00:18:07,568
then Bob uses Alice's
public key to verify and

215
00:18:07,568 --> 00:18:12,627
it looks like this,
that, you're using this

216
00:18:12,627 --> 00:18:17,964
message comes down here,
it gets transmitted.

217
00:18:17,964 --> 00:18:21,241
Now, to ensure that it comes from Alice,

218
00:18:21,241 --> 00:18:25,747
Alice would use her private
key to encrypt that message.

219
00:18:25,747 --> 00:18:32,628
That creates the combination of the hash
and encrypt will create this signature.

220
00:18:32,628 --> 00:18:38,170
And since the public key widely available,

221
00:18:38,170 --> 00:18:44,309
can be used to decrypt that and
perform the hash,

222
00:18:44,309 --> 00:18:49,552
only Alice's private key would have been

223
00:18:49,552 --> 00:18:56,911
able to be one that could
generated the actual signature.

224
00:18:56,911 --> 00:19:00,066
because if it was anything else,

225
00:19:00,066 --> 00:19:05,586
when you went to use the public
key to decrypt it and

226
00:19:05,586 --> 00:19:10,581
then compare it with
the hash of the message,

227
00:19:10,581 --> 00:19:13,877
it would be kind of different.

228
00:19:13,877 --> 00:19:16,030
It would be different.

229
00:19:16,030 --> 00:19:21,386
So that's a digital signature
it's basically using the property

230
00:19:21,386 --> 00:19:26,551
of asymmetric encryption, but
doing it a little differently,

231
00:19:26,551 --> 00:19:31,444
where you're using the private
key on the encryption side.

232
00:19:31,444 --> 00:19:34,770
But this allows you to say,

233
00:19:34,770 --> 00:19:39,401
this only could have come from Alice.

234
00:19:39,401 --> 00:19:43,314
All right, so
the next thing is Authentication.

235
00:19:43,314 --> 00:19:48,123
So we want to ensure that
you're only communicating

236
00:19:48,123 --> 00:19:52,398
with who you expect to
be communicating with.

237
00:19:52,398 --> 00:19:57,056
And so, as an illustration,
say we have Not Alice, and

238
00:19:57,056 --> 00:20:02,525
Not Alice is distributing a public key,
pretending to be Alice.

239
00:20:02,525 --> 00:20:06,444
And Bob gets that and says,
this is Alice's public key.

240
00:20:06,444 --> 00:20:10,833
Well, now it doesn't matter what
we saw in the previous slide,

241
00:20:10,833 --> 00:20:13,692
because Not Alice could
use that private key.

242
00:20:13,692 --> 00:20:18,119
And then Bob would think,
yeah, this is Alice.

243
00:20:18,119 --> 00:20:23,236
All right, so
this is where authentication comes in.

244
00:20:23,236 --> 00:20:29,419
So to do this, we use something
called a Digital Certificate.

245
00:20:29,419 --> 00:20:35,204
And this is something that combined
a public key and an identity.

246
00:20:35,204 --> 00:20:39,261
And the definition of identity
depends on the context.

247
00:20:39,261 --> 00:20:45,476
But shown here is something where
you have your name it's Alice,

248
00:20:45,476 --> 00:20:50,452
the Organization, whatever,
AliceCo in the USA.

249
00:20:50,452 --> 00:20:56,221
The combination of these get
bound together of the extra

250
00:20:56,221 --> 00:21:02,616
identity information and
the public key get bound together.

251
00:21:02,616 --> 00:21:08,442
And then Bob could validate when
I'm using a public key well,

252
00:21:08,442 --> 00:21:12,936
this extra information
says that it's Alice.

253
00:21:12,936 --> 00:21:18,803
All right, so one problem with that
is the integrity of the certificate.

254
00:21:18,803 --> 00:21:22,630
So an attacker could
modify this certificate.

255
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:26,055
So put in Not Alice's public key, but

256
00:21:26,055 --> 00:21:31,736
keep the rest of the information and
still pretend to be Alice.

257
00:21:31,736 --> 00:21:37,619
So to solve that,
we use a Certificate Authority.

258
00:21:37,619 --> 00:21:42,032
And this is some trusted
authority that can verify

259
00:21:42,032 --> 00:21:46,760
Alice's identity and
use its own private key there,

260
00:21:46,760 --> 00:21:52,239
that is the private key of
the certificate authority to sign.

261
00:21:52,239 --> 00:21:55,683
And if you recall,
what does it mean to sign something?

262
00:21:55,683 --> 00:22:00,813
That means that it only could
have come from that if you

263
00:22:00,813 --> 00:22:05,830
have the public key of that entity,
the certificate

264
00:22:05,830 --> 00:22:10,848
authority, well,
then when it signed something,

265
00:22:10,848 --> 00:22:16,917
it could have only been signed
by that certificate authority.

266
00:22:16,917 --> 00:22:22,780
Now, because it uses that public
key of the certificate authority,

267
00:22:22,780 --> 00:22:27,028
anybody can verify
the digital certificate using

268
00:22:27,028 --> 00:22:32,401
the certificate authority's public key or
CA's public key.

269
00:22:32,401 --> 00:22:33,751
Now, of course,

270
00:22:33,751 --> 00:22:39,691
this means that the CA that they have to
have their public key has to be trusted.

271
00:22:39,691 --> 00:22:43,361
And how this is commonly handled
is these are things that

272
00:22:43,361 --> 00:22:45,950
are like pre-installed in a browser.

273
00:22:45,950 --> 00:22:51,136
So there's some commonly
accepted certificate authorities,

274
00:22:51,136 --> 00:22:56,907
when you install software, you do that
kind of in a more manual process.

275
00:22:56,907 --> 00:23:00,346
But now what the power of that is,
is Alice, so

276
00:23:00,346 --> 00:23:05,035
I don't have to pre-install Alice or
manually do something.

277
00:23:05,035 --> 00:23:08,814
If Alice went to
the certificate authority,

278
00:23:08,814 --> 00:23:12,195
was able to prove her identity to the CA,

279
00:23:12,195 --> 00:23:16,876
and how that happens is going to
be in different contexts.

280
00:23:16,876 --> 00:23:23,372
And we'll show a couple examples
later in subsequent videos.

281
00:23:23,372 --> 00:23:28,214
And then that CA says, yep,
that happened, and now it signs it and

282
00:23:28,214 --> 00:23:30,648
now it's available to anybody.

283
00:23:30,648 --> 00:23:34,617
And now this is bound together and

284
00:23:34,617 --> 00:23:38,314
signed and verified by the CA.

285
00:23:38,314 --> 00:23:42,078
All right, so that ends kind of
the overview of the basics.

286
00:23:42,078 --> 00:23:47,208
We'll see these in practice for
various approaches to handling

287
00:23:47,208 --> 00:23:52,058
the security problems that we
identified in the last video.

288
00:23:52,058 --> 00:23:53,335
All right, thanks.